WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:04.960
So context engineering, possibly the single most popular

00:04.960 --> 00:08.000
expression in our world last year.

00:08.000 --> 00:12.800
Context engineering, so look, the output of an LLM,

00:12.800 --> 00:16.640
we know it's based entirely on the input.

00:16.640 --> 00:18.719
It's stateless, you give it an input,

00:18.719 --> 00:21.199
it produces an output, the most likely tokens

00:21.199 --> 00:23.879
to follow that input, and that input is sometimes known

00:23.879 --> 00:26.519
as the context,'because it's all the contextual

00:26.519 --> 00:32.060
information that we're giving the LLM that it can use to generate the output.

00:32.060 --> 00:36.340
Getting that input right, getting the context right, that's all we've got.

00:36.340 --> 00:37.340
That's it.

00:37.340 --> 00:39.560
The output is only based on the input.

00:39.560 --> 00:43.079
So to say getting this right is everything, it's almost like obvious that of course it's

00:43.079 --> 00:44.079
everything.

00:44.079 --> 00:45.079
That's all it has.

00:45.079 --> 00:46.079
It's got that input.

00:46.079 --> 00:49.920
And so getting it right is what this game is all about.

00:50.719 --> 00:55.359
We used to call that prompt engineering. Remember when prompt engineering a couple of years ago

00:55.359 --> 00:59.920
was like a job that people could have and it was the idea that you had to figure out the best way

00:59.920 --> 01:05.840
to prompt an LLM. But really that term prompt engineering has evolved into this bigger term

01:05.840 --> 01:11.159
context engineering which is recognizing it's not just about the prompt, it's all other

01:11.159 --> 01:16.560
things like the kind of tools you give it and other things that surround it to make

01:16.560 --> 01:22.599
sure that you're doing the best job you possibly can do to give the LLM all the right

01:22.599 --> 01:29.400
information in the context so that the output is most likely to achieve your goal. That whole

01:29.400 --> 01:33.280
set of techniques is what's known as context engineering.

01:33.280 --> 01:35.280
And there were a lot of different components

01:35.280 --> 01:37.879
of what goes into this input context,

01:37.879 --> 01:40.079
this input to an LLM.

01:40.079 --> 01:41.760
First of them, the first thing,

01:41.760 --> 01:43.680
and one of the first things you learn about

01:43.680 --> 01:46.000
when you're calling an LLM directly,

01:46.000 --> 01:47.599
is there's a part of the prompt,

01:47.599 --> 01:50.480
part of the text input that's known as the system prompt

01:50.480 --> 01:52.560
that is the most crucial,

01:52.560 --> 01:54.859
the most general information

01:54.859 --> 01:57.579
that frames the overall situation.

01:57.579 --> 02:00.219
What role is this LLM playing?

02:00.219 --> 02:02.180
What's its overall job?

02:02.180 --> 02:04.859
Is it an agent that's responsible for writing code?

02:04.859 --> 02:07.739
Is it an agent that is a customer support agent

02:07.739 --> 02:08.900
for an airline?

02:08.900 --> 02:11.500
Whatever it is, that overall framing,

02:11.500 --> 02:13.259
including the tone it should take

02:13.259 --> 02:14.819
and the approach it should use,

02:14.819 --> 02:20.280
that goes into something called the system prompt, which usually is at the very beginning of the information that is

02:20.280 --> 02:22.360
sent to the LLM.

02:22.360 --> 02:27.599
There's then a set of descriptions of the different tools that it has access to, a bit

02:27.599 --> 02:32.319
like the way I said that if it put the word Python and then something, it would be saying,

02:32.319 --> 02:34.280
I want to run that in Python.

02:34.280 --> 02:39.919
And that descriptions of the different tools, that's also part of the context.

02:39.919 --> 02:41.520
That's what might go in there next.

02:41.520 --> 02:44.199
And of course, the more tools you want it to have access to,

02:44.199 --> 02:46.800
the more space you will fill up with these tools.

02:46.800 --> 02:50.039
And some people might say that those tool descriptions

02:50.039 --> 02:52.080
are actually part of the system prompt.

02:52.080 --> 02:53.479
And that's really a definitional thing.

02:53.479 --> 02:55.560
It doesn't particularly matter if you think of them

02:55.560 --> 02:58.960
as being part of the same thing or just two separate chunks.

02:58.960 --> 03:00.639
It's more or just about how you label it.

03:00.639 --> 03:04.800
But in for this diagram anyway, I'm saying there is the overall framing

03:04.800 --> 03:10.240
followed by the tools. And then there's something that is sometimes called the memory.

03:10.240 --> 03:16.879
And this is basically information that you want to persist across potentially multiple

03:16.879 --> 03:23.599
different conversations. Some people call this long term memory or medium term memory, but this is typically general

03:24.000 --> 03:28.879
Resources that can change over time, but the you'd want to always be able to come back to and

03:29.280 --> 03:32.240
Then there is the big part of it

03:32.240 --> 03:36.919
Which is basically the the the actual input that you're sending to the LLM

03:37.319 --> 03:45.960
But remember because of the this way that we have this this trick that that allows us to give the illusion of a memory. What we

03:45.960 --> 03:51.379
send can't just be the most recent message that we want to send the LLM. It's got to be

03:51.379 --> 03:56.240
the entire conversation so far. It's got to be the very first message that we sent

03:56.240 --> 04:01.060
and then its reply and then our follow-on message and its reply to that. So all of the

04:01.060 --> 04:07.400
messages that formed this conversation to the LLM. All need to fit next in the context.

04:07.400 --> 04:09.520
You'll need to be squeezed in next.

04:09.520 --> 04:12.199
And that also needs to include any reasoning

04:12.199 --> 04:13.699
that the LLM has done,

04:13.699 --> 04:15.639
any of these tokens that it generates

04:15.639 --> 04:17.800
to describe its thought process.

04:17.800 --> 04:20.560
There are sometimes when you can actually not include that,

04:20.560 --> 04:21.600
but generally speaking,

04:21.600 --> 04:23.480
that does tend to get included here.

04:23.480 --> 04:26.720
And it's replies, and then if it's generated code,

04:26.720 --> 04:28.959
then that code that it's generated needs to go here.

04:28.959 --> 04:34.480
If it's called tools, then the tools that it's decided to call and the output from calling those

04:34.480 --> 04:40.480
tools, that all has to go in here. All this information forms the input and when it's generating

04:40.480 --> 04:52.639
the output, it will be generating output that's consistent with this entire conversation history history of the full conversation so far. So all of that makes up the single

04:52.639 --> 04:57.959
input to the LLM because it's stateless and needs to get this entire package

04:57.959 --> 05:03.160
that you see here in order to be able to generate the most likely next token

05:03.160 --> 05:06.839
that will be consistent with our objective. And there's something else. In

05:06.839 --> 05:14.839
the coding agents that we're going to be working with. There is a special thing that gets included in there too, which is really, I guess, part

05:14.839 --> 05:15.839
of memory.

05:15.839 --> 05:20.319
But I'm going to show it as a separate line item, if you will, a separate thing that gets

05:20.319 --> 05:21.800
shoved in here.

05:21.800 --> 05:24.279
And it is a special file.

05:24.279 --> 05:28.040
And it's often called agents.md.

05:28.040 --> 05:30.839
And you may well know about this already, but we'll be looking at it.

05:30.839 --> 05:36.680
And we'll be talking a lot about what makes a good one and a bad one and so on. And you can think of this as being like part of the memory.

05:36.680 --> 05:38.560
It's a resource that persists.

05:38.560 --> 05:40.759
Or you can say this is something extra

05:40.759 --> 05:42.660
in addition to the other parts of memory.

05:42.660 --> 05:46.240
This agents.md is something special.

05:46.240 --> 05:48.079
And of course, if you're working with Cloud Code,

05:48.079 --> 05:50.040
it's in fact, claud.md.

05:50.040 --> 05:52.800
And with anti-gravity, it's germany.md.

05:52.800 --> 05:54.560
But these are all what we generally think of

05:54.560 --> 05:57.000
as the agents.md files.

05:57.000 --> 06:04.000
And these are files that we particularly use with coding agents that describe special things

06:04.000 --> 06:09.000
about the project we're working on, particular coding standards or things about our objective,

06:09.000 --> 06:14.000
something that we want to be in the memory of the AI agent while it's writing code for us.

06:14.000 --> 06:21.319
That is agents.md, a special file we write that gets shoved in the context every single time.

06:21.319 --> 06:23.300
Ah, I can hear you correcting me there.

06:23.300 --> 06:25.300
You know that it's not every single time.

06:25.300 --> 06:26.540
It's more complicated than that.

06:26.540 --> 06:28.199
But never fear, we will get to that.

06:28.199 --> 06:29.100
We will get to that.

06:29.100 --> 06:31.500
But for now, consider it to be every single time.

06:31.500 --> 06:34.100
All right, so that is the context.

06:34.100 --> 06:37.300
That's what context engineering is all about.

06:37.300 --> 06:40.639
And as you probably also know, but there's no harm in us going through it

06:40.639 --> 06:48.920
again, there is, of course, this idea of a context window, which is to say that an LLM

06:48.920 --> 06:57.360
has a limit to how many tokens can be fit in this context before it runs out of room.

06:57.360 --> 07:02.720
Because an LLM is designed to be able to look back a number of different tokens as it's forming its predicted

07:02.720 --> 07:08.519
next tokens and each LLM can only look back so far that they are only large enough to

07:08.519 --> 07:11.079
look back a certain amount of tokens.

07:11.079 --> 07:17.759
And if you try and pass in more tokens than its maximum context window, it will fail.

07:17.759 --> 07:19.199
That is considered a break.

07:19.199 --> 07:22.000
It can't handle it and it will be an error.

07:22.000 --> 07:25.079
It's the maximum number of tokens that it can examine

07:25.079 --> 07:28.399
in its input in order to predict the next tokens.

07:28.399 --> 07:30.360
That's called, that's sometimes just called

07:30.360 --> 07:33.959
the context window or the context window limit,

07:33.959 --> 07:37.720
but it is the maximum size of this context

07:37.720 --> 07:39.000
that's passed in.

07:39.000 --> 07:43.079
And so it needs to fit within this context window.

07:43.079 --> 07:45.600
And that's something which many of us

07:45.600 --> 07:47.680
agonize over constantly.

07:47.680 --> 07:53.279
And people obsess over this context limit, the fact that you can't go over it.

07:53.279 --> 07:58.459
But in fact, it's worth keeping in mind that in a lot of cases, just having too much

07:58.459 --> 08:03.800
in the context, even if you're not yet at the limit, it can still cause performance

08:03.800 --> 08:05.240
to degrade.

08:05.240 --> 08:08.519
And I'm using performance there, not in the kind of speed sense,

08:08.519 --> 08:11.680
not performance like we software people sometimes say,

08:11.680 --> 08:13.120
I'm talking about the accuracy,

08:13.120 --> 08:15.920
the quality of the results, that kind of performance.

08:15.920 --> 08:18.959
That can start to degrade, you get poorer results,

08:18.959 --> 08:21.120
you start to lose coherence,

08:21.120 --> 08:23.639
it starts to forget things as you fill up

08:23.639 --> 08:25.079
the context more and more.

08:25.079 --> 08:27.519
So even if you're not yet at the limit,

08:27.519 --> 08:30.779
even if you haven't filled up the context window, you might start

08:30.779 --> 08:35.700
to see degrading performance if you're shoving too much in the context.

08:35.700 --> 08:40.240
And conversely, you tend to get the best performance at the very start of your conversation

08:40.240 --> 08:45.200
when the context is super empty and it's only got a little bit to pay attention to.

08:45.200 --> 08:47.539
And so that's something to be very cognizant of.

08:47.539 --> 08:54.879
It's not just a binary, have you got too much, it fails. There's also a sense of less is more when it comes

08:54.879 --> 09:00.080
to filling up the context. Okay, now I've been saying that the LLM fails when the context

09:00.080 --> 09:04.440
window gets filled up and you're probably thinking, well, I don't think it fails. I think

09:04.440 --> 09:09.799
it does compacting. You've probably experienced compacting before. That's actually a feature

09:09.799 --> 09:15.039
built in to things like Cloud Code, which is that rather than allowing it to fail, it detects

09:15.039 --> 09:20.080
when the context window is almost filled up and it runs this process that essentially looks back

09:20.080 --> 09:27.039
at the whole conversation history and then summarizes it and replaces everything with a

09:27.039 --> 09:33.279
little summary of what was there before, freeing up lots of space in the context history, in the

09:33.279 --> 09:37.039
conversation history. And this is something which which is very common

09:37.039 --> 09:43.360
and it's also something which is feared by people like you and me a lot because it changes the

09:43.360 --> 09:48.240
game suddenly. The challenge with compacting is that we are trusting the all-alam to do a good

09:48.240 --> 09:52.799
job of figuring out what's important and what's not important from the conversation history

09:52.799 --> 09:57.919
and often it does a good job but sometimes it doesn't sometimes it misses an important thing

09:59.759 --> 10:04.720
but sometimes it doesn't. Sometimes it misses an important thing that we care a lot about, that we told it about during the conversation, that gets removed when it's summarizing. And as a

10:04.720 --> 10:09.759
result, the LLM appears to forget something that we've told it, or it makes the same mistake

10:09.759 --> 10:16.080
twice, which is super frustrating. And because of this, people have tended to fear the compactor,

10:16.080 --> 10:21.600
don't like it when it compacts. And often, you'll find the people that I do this a lot myself.

10:21.600 --> 10:27.120
Rather than let it compact, I will look at what's happened before, I will stop the agent.

10:27.120 --> 10:33.679
And then I will rewrite my file like agents.md that I mentioned before and then started up again

10:33.679 --> 10:38.639
fresh, making sure that I've kept careful note of everything that's happened that it needs to know

10:38.639 --> 10:42.799
about. So people do tend to fear the compacting process

10:42.799 --> 10:45.480
and like to stop it and refresh it and rewrite it

10:45.480 --> 10:48.960
because there's a lack of trust for how compacting works.

10:48.960 --> 10:50.240
There's a particular fear as well

10:50.240 --> 10:52.279
for if it's going to run compacting

10:52.279 --> 10:54.360
whilst it's in the middle of doing something.

10:54.360 --> 10:55.919
And that's something that you can get around

10:55.919 --> 10:57.799
by forcing it to compact at a good time.

10:57.799 --> 10:59.440
And that's a bit different.

10:59.440 --> 11:02.919
But I'm talking about is just a general fear of compacting.

11:02.919 --> 11:04.360
But the truth of the matter is,

11:04.360 --> 11:06.320
compacting has got a lot better

11:06.320 --> 11:08.360
in a just recent history.

11:08.360 --> 11:10.879
And now as of 2026,

11:10.879 --> 11:13.840
there is less reason to fear the compactor.

11:13.840 --> 11:16.080
It does a really good job.

11:16.080 --> 11:18.840
So I am still of the old guard

11:18.840 --> 11:21.639
that I do tend to still like to stop

11:21.639 --> 11:23.960
and summarize my agents.md.

11:23.960 --> 11:26.720
But I know that some of that is my stubbornness at this

11:26.720 --> 11:30.519
point and that often you can just trust the compactor.

11:30.519 --> 11:32.840
And so I'm here to tell you, don't be like me.

11:32.840 --> 11:39.139
At least start out by trusting the compactor, trust compacting, let it do its thing and it

11:39.139 --> 11:44.720
will often do a really fine job of summarizing the conversation history, keeping relevant

11:44.720 --> 11:50.919
stuff that it needs to remember and freeing up tons of space for more conversation.

11:50.919 --> 11:53.360
That's what it does and it tends to do it well.

11:53.360 --> 11:57.779
And maybe one more point to mention on context windows is just to tell you the context window

11:57.779 --> 12:00.779
size of different major models.

12:00.779 --> 12:15.539
So open AIs, GPT 5.2 that we used yesterday. yesterday, that has a context window of 400,000 tokens, 400,000 tokens, Claude, the

12:15.539 --> 12:24.059
Claude Sonnet 4.5, Claude Opus 4.5, that has 200,000 tokens, so about half. And Gemini,

12:24.059 --> 12:30.059
and the Gemini in Antigravity from Google, that has a context window of a million tokens,

12:30.059 --> 12:32.559
a million tokens, much greater.

12:32.559 --> 12:34.480
So these are the limits of you.

12:34.480 --> 12:36.559
It can't go above that number.

12:36.559 --> 12:41.519
But it's again worth pointing out that you'll see degrading performance as you start to put

12:41.519 --> 12:42.879
more in the context.

12:42.879 --> 12:49.000
So personally, I don't over obsess about these max context window sizes because I'm

12:49.000 --> 12:54.480
more interested in how is performance affected by putting more into the context.

12:54.480 --> 12:59.919
And I find across all the models that having less in the context gets better results.

